<h2>What's Included</h2>

<p>Guideline's platform includes several key modules. Availability by contract scope varies:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>AI Factory</strong>: Automated placement classification and media spend categorization from transactional billing data</li>
<li><strong>AI Agent (conversational)</strong>: Natural language interface for querying media spend benchmarks and planning scenarios</li>
<li><strong>Local Dynamics</strong> (launched March 2026): Local market spend intelligence and benchmarking</li>
<li><strong>MCP Server</strong> (launched March 2026): API integration for embedding Guideline data into agency tech stacks and AI workflows</li>
<li><strong>Global benchmark data</strong>: Rate and spend benchmarks drawn from ~$200B in real transactional data across 65 countries</li>
<li><strong>Cross-media planning</strong>: TV, digital, out-of-home, and print channel benchmarks</li>
</ul>

<h2>Typical Total Cost of Ownership</h2>

<p>Guideline is a strategic data platform, not a workflow tool — the value accrues through better-informed planning decisions. Consider these TCO factors:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Annual subscription</strong>: Enterprise contracts at this tier are typically annual or multi-year; spot purchases are not available</li>
<li><strong>User seat count</strong>: Large planning teams may require many named licenses; clarify seat pricing at volume</li>
<li><strong>Integration development</strong>: Connecting Guideline data via its MCP Server to agency systems may require technical resources; ask whether integration support is included</li>
<li><strong>Market scope</strong>: Pricing likely scales with the number of markets accessed; a global scope will cost more than a single-market deployment</li>
<li><strong>Training and onboarding</strong>: For a platform used at scale by large agencies, onboarding time is a real cost — confirm what's provided</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who It's Best For / Not For</h2>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Global and regional media agencies, holding company planning teams, and media owners that need transaction-based benchmark data for competitive rate intelligence. Particularly strong for TV, out-of-home, and print planning contexts where rate transparency matters most.</p>

<p><strong>Not ideal for:</strong> Small independent agencies, advertisers without significant media buying scale, or teams primarily focused on digital/programmatic channels where other platforms offer stronger workflow tooling. Guideline's differentiation is its transactional data depth — if you don't need benchmark rates, the value proposition weakens.</p>

<h2>How to Negotiate</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Define the geographic scope first</strong> — A global contract is far more expensive than a regional one; start by scoping the markets you actively plan in</li>
<li><strong>Ask about data-only vs. platform access pricing</strong> — If your primary need is benchmark data (not AI agents or planning workflow), a lighter data license may be available</li>
<li><strong>Benchmark against Kantar SRDS and MediaRadar</strong> — Competitive intelligence tools overlap in some use cases and can provide negotiating context</li>
<li><strong>Negotiate multi-year terms for better rates</strong> — Guideline's data assets make switching costly; vendors in this position often offer meaningful discounts for longer commitments</li>
</ol>

