<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Does Guideline publish pricing?</h3>
<p>No. Guideline does not publish pricing on its website. All contracts are custom-negotiated through direct sales engagement. Given the platform's enterprise positioning, expect a multi-step sales process including a requirements conversation and scoped quote.</p>

<h3>Who are Guideline's typical customers?</h3>
<p>Guideline is used by approximately 90% of the world's top global media companies. Its primary customers are media agencies, holding company planning groups, and media owners who need access to transaction-based spend benchmarks across TV, digital, and traditional media.</p>

<h3>How is Guideline's data different from published rate cards?</h3>
<p>Guideline's data comes from actual agency billing systems — real transaction prices, not rate cards or estimated spend. This makes it more accurate for competitive benchmarking than publisher-provided rate information or estimated data from tools like Kantar Ad Intel.</p>

<h3>What's new in Guideline in 2026?</h3>
<p>Guideline launched its MCP Server (March 2026), enabling API integration with agency AI workflows and tech stacks. Local Dynamics (also March 2026) adds local market spend intelligence. The AI Agent conversational interface continues to expand in scope.</p>

<h3>Is there a minimum spend or company size requirement?</h3>
<p>Not publicly stated, but Guideline's positioning and pricing structure strongly favor large agencies and media groups. Independent agencies with limited media spend under management are unlikely to find the platform cost-effective relative to lighter alternatives.</p>

<h3>Does Guideline integrate with other media planning tools?</h3>
<p>Yes — Guideline's March 2026 MCP Server launch specifically enables integration with agency AI systems and existing tech stacks. Integration scope and support terms are negotiated as part of the enterprise contract.</p>

